-- D8's persistence safety: what may not reach an artifact.
--
-- WI-A13, stage 6. D8 says, in one sentence that carries two obligations:
--
--   "Programs contain synthetic values only. Environment maps and interaction
--    artifacts must reject or redact secret-shaped/live credentials before
--    persistence."
--
-- This module is the detector and the redactor. It holds NO encoder, no file
-- path and no FS effect, for the same structural reason stage 2 moved
-- `Interaction` down into `src/core/dst_interaction`: the check has to be
-- callable from the place bytes are produced, and it must not be able to
-- produce bytes itself. `src/core/dst_persistence` composes the two, and the
-- ORDERING obligation — redaction before persistence — is structural there
-- rather than asserted, because encoding a program that still carries a
-- credential is a REFUSAL and not a string.
--
-- It imports nothing but std and `dst_interaction`, which is std-only. That is
-- load-bearing rather than tidy: A14 records interaction artifacts at the port
-- seam, and `ports.ail` cannot import a module that names `ExecutionManifest`
-- without dragging the whole `dst_profile` closure into the production driver's
-- import graph.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE DESIGN DECISION, AND IT IS THE ONE S8's COMPLEMENT FORCES
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- A redactor that keys on NAMES certifies nothing about this driver, and the
-- proof is one grep away: not one of the seven keys `driver_env_keys()` derives
-- from the driver's source contains SECRET, TOKEN, PASSWORD, KEY or CREDENTIAL.
--
--   MOTOKO_SESSION_ID  MOTOKO_PERSIST_RETRIES  MOTOKO_RETRY_STREAM_ERROR
--   OPENAI_BASE_URL    MOTOKO_HEADLESS         MOTOKO_CAPTURE_FAILED_PAYLOAD
--   MOTOKO_TOOL_TIMEOUT_MS
--
-- A name-only detector is green on every one of them while
-- `OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://svc:sk-live-…@api.example.com/v1` walks straight
-- into the artifact. That URL is a "live credential" by D8's own words and is
-- the exact surface this driver has. So detection has TWO INDEPENDENT AXES and
-- the value axis is the load-bearing one:
--
--   1. NAME-SHAPED — the key announces itself. Cheap, and it catches the keys a
--      profile that installs extensions will add. It catches none of the seven.
--   2. VALUE-SHAPED — the value is a credential whatever it is called. Provider
--      token prefixes, URL userinfo, PEM private-key blocks, JWTs, and opaque
--      high-entropy tokens.
--
-- `scripts/dst/program_persistence_dst.ail` asserts both halves of that claim
-- executably: that every driver key is name-clean (so axis 1 is provably
-- insufficient here) and that a credential placed under one of those clean
-- names is still caught. Asserting it is the point — S7's corollary is that a
-- fixture's stated justification is itself a claim, and this one would rot the
-- moment somebody adds `MOTOKO_API_TOKEN` to the driver.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE TENSION D8 CREATES AND DOES NOT RESOLVE, CARRIED IN A TYPE
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- "Reject or redact" is offered as a free choice, and it is not one, because
-- THE ENVIRONMENT MAP IS A REPLAY INPUT. `InitialWorld.synthetic_environment`
-- is what `world_state_of` re-seeds the world from; an environment read's
-- outcome payload is what the driver actually receives. Redacting either
-- changes the trajectory of the replay, so a "redacted" program can be a
-- program that no longer reproduces the failure it was preserved for — while
-- every count still balances and the artifact still decodes.
--
-- That is not a reason to leave credentials in an artifact. It is a reason not
-- to let the choice be silent. Every `Redaction` therefore carries a
-- `ReplayImpact`, and the two values are not decorative:
--
--   RedactionDemoted          — the difference this introduces lands in D2's
--                               DEMOTED set, so regression replay records it
--                               and continues, and strict replay fails closed
--                               with a named mismatch. Nothing is silent in
--                               either mode. Request projections only.
--   RedactionChangesTrajectory — the value is CONSUMED by the driver, so the
--                               replay may diverge. Environment values and
--                               outcome payloads. `redaction_is_reportable`
--                               is true for these and the persistence path
--                               reports them rather than swallowing them.
--
-- The resolution this module takes for PROGRAMS is D8's own first sentence:
-- programs contain synthetic values only, so the correct handling of a
-- credential in a program is REJECTION — the program is wrong, not the
-- artifact. Redaction exists for interaction artifacts recorded from a live
-- run, where a credential can legitimately arrive, and there its trajectory
-- cost is reported.

module src/core/dst_secrets

import std/list as List (length)
import std/string as Str (
  length, toUpper, contains, startsWith, substring, find, foldChars, split
)
import src/core/dst_interaction (
  IdentityBody,
  ProviderIdentity, ToolIdentity, ApprovalIdentity, ExtensionEffectIdentity,
  EnvironmentReadIdentity, RandomDrawIdentity, ClockAdvanceIdentity,
  FileWriteIdentity, FileRemoveIdentity,
  CausalIdentity,
  StreamChunkRecord,
  TimedOutcome,
  Interaction
)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Where a credential was found, and why it is one
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- The POSITION, named precisely enough that a report points at one place. A
-- finding that says only "a secret is somewhere in this program" is a finding
-- nobody can act on, and the author of the program is the last person who will
-- find it by reading.
export type SecretSite
  = EnvKeySite(string)                  -- environment map entry, by key
  | InitialWorldSite(string)            -- an initial-world field, by name
  | ManifestSite(string)                -- an execution-manifest field, by name
  | RequestProjectionSite(int)          -- interaction ordinal
  | PayloadSite(int)                    -- interaction ordinal
  | ChunkSite(int, int)                 -- interaction ordinal, chunk index
  | IdentityComponentSite(int, string)  -- interaction ordinal, component name

-- WHY it is a credential. Kept separate from the site because the two vary
-- independently, and because the reason is what tells a reader whether the
-- detector was right — an `OpaqueHighEntropy` finding on a synthetic value is a
-- false positive worth seeing, and a `CredentialBearingName` on a blank value
-- is not.
export type SecretReason
  = CredentialBearingName(string)   -- the token in the key that matched
  | ProviderTokenLiteral(string)    -- the credential prefix that matched
  | UrlUserinfo                     -- scheme://user:token@host
  | PrivateKeyBlock                 -- a PEM private key
  | JsonWebToken                    -- eyJ… . … . …
  | OpaqueHighEntropy(int)          -- length of an unbroken credential-alphabet run

export type SecretFinding = { site: SecretSite, reason: SecretReason }

export pure func secret_site_label(s: SecretSite) -> string {
  match s {
    EnvKeySite(k) => "environment map entry '${k}'",
    InitialWorldSite(f) => "initial_world.${f}",
    ManifestSite(f) => "manifest.${f}",
    RequestProjectionSite(o) => "interaction #${o}'s request projection",
    PayloadSite(o) => "interaction #${o}'s outcome payload",
    ChunkSite(o, n) => "interaction #${o}'s stream chunk ${n}",
    IdentityComponentSite(o, c) => "interaction #${o}'s identity component '${c}'"
  }
}

-- The stable rule slug. These are what the acceptance rows assert, for the
-- reason stage 1 gave for `program_rule`: a row that asserts a non-empty
-- finding list passes against a detector that reports everything.
export pure func secret_rule(f: SecretFinding) -> string {
  match f.reason {
    CredentialBearingName(_) => "secret-credential-bearing-name",
    ProviderTokenLiteral(_) => "secret-provider-token-literal",
    UrlUserinfo => "secret-url-userinfo",
    PrivateKeyBlock => "secret-private-key-block",
    JsonWebToken => "secret-json-web-token",
    OpaqueHighEntropy(_) => "secret-opaque-high-entropy"
  }
}

export pure func secret_message(f: SecretFinding) -> string {
  let where = secret_site_label(f.site);
  match f.reason {
    CredentialBearingName(t) =>
      "[secret-credential-bearing-name] ${where} names '${t}'; D8 requires environment maps and interaction artifacts to reject or redact secret-shaped credentials BEFORE persistence, and a key that announces itself is the cheapest half of that",
    ProviderTokenLiteral(p) =>
      "[secret-provider-token-literal] ${where} holds a value beginning '${p}', which is a live provider credential prefix; D8 says programs contain SYNTHETIC values only, so this is a defect in the program and not in the artifact",
    UrlUserinfo =>
      "[secret-url-userinfo] ${where} holds a URL carrying userinfo (scheme://user:token@host); this is the shape the driver's own OPENAI_BASE_URL can take, and NO key-name rule reaches it — the key is name-clean and the credential is in the value",
    PrivateKeyBlock =>
      "[secret-private-key-block] ${where} holds a PEM private-key block; a program carrying one is not a synthetic program and must not be persisted",
    JsonWebToken =>
      "[secret-json-web-token] ${where} holds a JSON Web Token; the signature makes it a live bearer credential regardless of what the claims say",
    OpaqueHighEntropy(n) =>
      "[secret-opaque-high-entropy] ${where} holds an unbroken ${n}-character run of credential-alphabet text with mixed case and digits; that is the residual shape a named rule misses, and D8 asks for rejection or redaction rather than a judgement call at persistence time"
  }
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Axis 1: the name
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Tokens that make a key name self-declaring. Deliberately NOT tuned to this
-- driver: the point of the acceptance row that checks `driver_env_keys()`
-- against this list is that the intersection is currently EMPTY, so this axis
-- is carried for the profiles that install extensions and supply their own
-- keys, and the value axis is what protects the driver as it stands.
export pure func credential_name_tokens() -> [string] {
  ["SECRET", "TOKEN", "PASSWORD", "PASSWD", "CREDENTIAL", "APIKEY", "API_KEY",
   "PRIVATE_KEY", "ACCESS_KEY", "SECRET_KEY", "AUTH", "BEARER", "SIGNATURE",
   "SESSION_KEY", "PASSPHRASE"]
}

pure func first_name_token(tokens: [string], upper: string) -> [string] {
  match tokens {
    [] => [],
    t :: rest => if Str.contains(upper, t) then [t] else first_name_token(rest, upper)
  }
}

-- Whether a key name declares itself, and which token said so. Returns a list
-- rather than an Option so that it composes with the value axis by `++` and so
-- that "no finding" is the empty list everywhere in this module.
export pure func name_axis(key: string) -> [string] {
  first_name_token(credential_name_tokens(), Str.toUpper(key))
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Axis 2: the value
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Live credential prefixes. Each is a published, issuer-assigned prefix rather
-- than a guess, which is what makes a match evidence instead of a heuristic.
export pure func credential_value_prefixes() -> [string] {
  ["sk-", "sk_live_", "sk_test_", "pk_live_", "rk_live_",
   "ghp_", "gho_", "ghu_", "ghs_", "github_pat_",
   "xoxb-", "xoxp-", "xapp-",
   "AKIA", "ASIA", "AIza", "ya29.",
   "Bearer ", "Basic ", "glpat-", "npm_", "dop_v1_", "shpat_"]
}

pure func first_value_prefix(ps: [string], s: string) -> [string] {
  match ps {
    [] => [],
    p :: rest => if Str.contains(s, p) then [p] else first_value_prefix(rest, s)
  }
}

-- A URL carrying userinfo. Checked on the AUTHORITY section only — the text
-- between "://" and the next "/" — because a bare "@" anywhere in a payload is
-- an email address far more often than a credential, and a detector whose false
-- positives outnumber its true ones gets switched off.
pure func has_url_userinfo(s: string) -> bool {
  let i = Str.find(s, "://");
  if i < 0 then false
  else {
    let after = Str.substring(s, i + 3, Str.length(s));
    let slash = Str.find(after, "/");
    let authority = if slash < 0 then after else Str.substring(after, 0, slash);
    let at = Str.find(authority, "@");
    if at < 0 then false
    else {
      let userinfo = Str.substring(authority, 0, at);
      -- `user:token@host` carries a credential; a bare `user@host` is a
      -- username, which D8 does not protect and which appears in ordinary URLs.
      Str.find(userinfo, ":") >= 0
    }
  }
}

pure func has_private_key_block(s: string) -> bool {
  Str.contains(s, "-----BEGIN") && Str.contains(s, "PRIVATE KEY")
}

-- A JWT: three dot-separated segments whose first is a base64url-encoded JSON
-- header, which in practice always begins "eyJ". Checking the segment count as
-- well as the prefix is what keeps it off ordinary prose containing "eyJ".
pure func has_jwt(s: string) -> bool {
  jwt_in_parts(Str.split(s, "."))
}

pure func jwt_in_parts(parts: [string]) -> bool {
  match parts {
    a :: b :: c :: _ =>
      if Str.startsWith(a, "eyJ") && Str.length(b) >= 8 && Str.length(c) >= 8 then true
      else jwt_in_parts(b :: c :: []),
    _ => false
  }
}

-- The residual detector: an unbroken run of credential-alphabet characters,
-- long, with upper case, lower case and a digit all present.
--
-- Every one of those conditions is doing work and the conjunction is what keeps
-- the false-positive rate low enough to be usable:
--
--   * UNBROKEN — prose has spaces, so a sentence cannot reach the threshold.
--   * length >= 32 — shorter than a real bearer token and longer than any
--     identifier this project's synthetic values use.
--   * MIXED CASE plus a digit — a 40-character lower-case git revision and a
--     64-character lower-case sha256 digest both fail it, and both appear in
--     every manifest this project persists. Without that clause the detector
--     rejects the manifest of every honest program.
export pure func opaque_run_threshold() -> int { 32 }

type Run = { best: int, cur: int, upper: bool, lower: bool, digit: bool,
             cur_upper: bool, cur_lower: bool, cur_digit: bool }

pure func in_credential_alphabet(c: string) -> bool {
  is_upper(c) || is_lower(c) || is_digit(c) || c == "+" || c == "/" || c == "="
    || c == "_" || c == "-" || c == "."
}

pure func is_upper(c: string) -> bool { c >= "A" && c <= "Z" }
pure func is_lower(c: string) -> bool { c >= "a" && c <= "z" }
pure func is_digit(c: string) -> bool { c >= "0" && c <= "9" }

pure func run_step(a: Run, c: string) -> Run {
  if in_credential_alphabet(c) then {
    let n = a.cur + 1;
    let u = a.cur_upper || is_upper(c);
    let l = a.cur_lower || is_lower(c);
    let d = a.cur_digit || is_digit(c);
    -- The run is only a CANDIDATE while all three classes are present; a run
    -- that is long but single-class (a hex digest) never becomes the best.
    if n > a.best && u && l && d then
      { a | cur: n, best: n, upper: u, lower: l, digit: d,
            cur_upper: u, cur_lower: l, cur_digit: d }
    else
      { a | cur: n, cur_upper: u, cur_lower: l, cur_digit: d }
  } else {
    { a | cur: 0, cur_upper: false, cur_lower: false, cur_digit: false }
  }
}

-- The longest qualifying run, or 0. Exported because the acceptance suite
-- asserts the NEGATIVE control on it directly: a git revision and a sha256
-- digest must both score 0, and if they ever stop doing so this detector
-- rejects every honest manifest.
export pure func longest_opaque_run(s: string) -> int {
  let r = Str.foldChars(run_step, { best: 0, cur: 0, upper: false, lower: false,
                                    digit: false, cur_upper: false,
                                    cur_lower: false, cur_digit: false }, s);
  r.best
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The scan
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Every reason one string is a credential, at one site. This is the single
-- place the two axes meet, and every caller below goes through it — a site the
-- scan reaches by a different route is a site whose rules can drift.
export pure func scan_text(site: SecretSite, s: string) -> [SecretFinding] {
  if s == "" then []
  else {
    let prefix = first_value_prefix(credential_value_prefixes(), s);
    let run = longest_opaque_run(s);
    (match prefix { [] => [], p :: _ => [{ site: site, reason: ProviderTokenLiteral(p) }] })
      ++ (if has_url_userinfo(s) then [{ site: site, reason: UrlUserinfo }] else [])
      ++ (if has_private_key_block(s) then [{ site: site, reason: PrivateKeyBlock }] else [])
      ++ (if has_jwt(s) then [{ site: site, reason: JsonWebToken }] else [])
      ++ (if run >= opaque_run_threshold() then [{ site: site, reason: OpaqueHighEntropy(run) }] else [])
  }
}

-- An environment map entry: BOTH axes, and the key is scanned as a value too.
-- A key is not usually a credential, but `scan_text` on it costs nothing and a
-- profile that derives keys at runtime can put one there.
export pure func scan_env_entry(e: { key: string, value: string }) -> [SecretFinding] {
  (match name_axis(e.key) {
     [] => [],
     t :: _ => [{ site: EnvKeySite(e.key), reason: CredentialBearingName(t) }]
   })
    ++ scan_text(EnvKeySite(e.key), e.value)
}

export pure func scan_env_map(es: [{ key: string, value: string }]) -> [SecretFinding] {
  match es {
    [] => [],
    e :: rest => scan_env_entry(e) ++ scan_env_map(rest)
  }
}

pure func scan_chunks(ord: int, cs: [StreamChunkRecord], n: int) -> [SecretFinding] {
  match cs {
    [] => [],
    c :: rest => scan_text(ChunkSite(ord, n), c.text) ++ scan_chunks(ord, rest, n + 1)
  }
}

-- The identity's own components. An environment read's KEY and a tool's
-- arguments-derived name both land here, and a credential in an identity is
-- worse than one in a payload: the identity is what replay compares on, so
-- redacting it changes what "the same logical request" means.
pure func scan_identity(id: CausalIdentity) -> [SecretFinding] {
  let o = id.ordinal;
  match id.body {
    ProviderIdentity(origin, _, model) =>
      scan_text(IdentityComponentSite(o, "provider origin"), origin)
        ++ scan_text(IdentityComponentSite(o, "provider model"), model),
    ToolIdentity(origin, call_id, name) =>
      scan_text(IdentityComponentSite(o, "tool origin"), origin)
        ++ scan_text(IdentityComponentSite(o, "tool call_id"), call_id)
        ++ scan_text(IdentityComponentSite(o, "tool name"), name),
    ApprovalIdentity(origin, call_id, name) =>
      scan_text(IdentityComponentSite(o, "approval origin"), origin)
        ++ scan_text(IdentityComponentSite(o, "approval call_id"), call_id)
        ++ scan_text(IdentityComponentSite(o, "approval name"), name),
    ExtensionEffectIdentity(ext_id, class_id, call_id) =>
      scan_text(IdentityComponentSite(o, "extension_effect ext_id"), ext_id)
        ++ scan_text(IdentityComponentSite(o, "extension_effect class_id"), class_id)
        ++ scan_text(IdentityComponentSite(o, "extension_effect call_id"), call_id),
    -- An env read's key gets the NAME axis as well, because this is the same
    -- surface `scan_env_entry` protects arriving through the interaction log.
    EnvironmentReadIdentity(key) =>
      (match name_axis(key) {
         [] => [],
         t :: _ => [{ site: IdentityComponentSite(o, "environment_read key"),
                      reason: CredentialBearingName(t) }]
       })
        ++ scan_text(IdentityComponentSite(o, "environment_read key"), key),
    RandomDrawIdentity(bounds) =>
      scan_text(IdentityComponentSite(o, "random_draw bounds"), bounds),
    ClockAdvanceIdentity => [],
    -- WI-D17. A path gets `scan_text` and NOT the `name_axis` treatment an env
    -- key gets. The env key earns that axis because a key NAME
    -- (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) is itself the evidence; a path name is not — the
    -- credential-bearing thing about a file is its CONTENT, and this class
    -- deliberately never records content. `recording_file_write` puts a byte
    -- LENGTH in the projection and nothing in the payload, which is why the
    -- scanner has nothing to do here beyond the ordinary text axis.
    FileWriteIdentity(path) =>
      scan_text(IdentityComponentSite(o, "file_write path"), path),
    FileRemoveIdentity(path) =>
      scan_text(IdentityComponentSite(o, "file_remove path"), path)
  }
}

export pure func scan_interaction(i: Interaction) -> [SecretFinding] {
  let o = i.identity.ordinal;
  scan_identity(i.identity)
    ++ scan_text(RequestProjectionSite(o), i.request_projection)
    ++ scan_text(PayloadSite(o), i.outcome.payload)
    ++ scan_chunks(o, i.outcome.chunks, 0)
}

export pure func scan_interactions(xs: [Interaction]) -> [SecretFinding] {
  match xs {
    [] => [],
    i :: rest => scan_interaction(i) ++ scan_interactions(rest)
  }
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Redaction, and the trajectory cost it cannot hide
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- See the module header. These are not two labels on one action; they are two
-- different consequences, and only one of them is safe to apply quietly.
export type ReplayImpact = RedactionDemoted | RedactionChangesTrajectory

export pure func replay_impact_id(r: ReplayImpact) -> string {
  match r {
    RedactionDemoted => "demoted",
    RedactionChangesTrajectory => "changes-trajectory"
  }
}

export type Redaction = { finding: SecretFinding, impact: ReplayImpact }

-- Which impact a site carries. This is a total function of the SITE rather
-- than of the reason, because what a replay consumes is a property of where
-- the value sits and not of why it was flagged.
export pure func site_impact(s: SecretSite) -> ReplayImpact {
  match s {
    -- `world_state_of` re-seeds the world from the environment map, and an
    -- environment read's payload IS what the driver receives.
    EnvKeySite(_) => RedactionChangesTrajectory,
    InitialWorldSite(_) => RedactionChangesTrajectory,
    -- D8 conditions its whole reproducibility promise on the recorded manifest,
    -- and D11 says a program promoted without one "is not a reproduction unit —
    -- it is a program nobody can state the meaning of". Redacting a manifest
    -- field is therefore at least as costly as redacting an environment value.
    ManifestSite(_) => RedactionChangesTrajectory,
    PayloadSite(_) => RedactionChangesTrajectory,
    ChunkSite(_, _) => RedactionChangesTrajectory,
    -- Replay compares identities EXACTLY in both modes, so a redacted identity
    -- component is a fatal mismatch rather than a demoted difference. It is not
    -- silently survivable and must not be classified as if it were.
    IdentityComponentSite(_, _) => RedactionChangesTrajectory,
    -- D2 demotes a request-projection difference: regression replay records it
    -- and continues, strict replay fails closed. Neither is silent, which is
    -- the whole distinction this type exists to make.
    RequestProjectionSite(_) => RedactionDemoted
  }
}

-- Whether a redaction must be REPORTED rather than applied quietly. True
-- exactly when the redaction can change what the replay does — which is D8's
-- unresolved tension, carried to the caller instead of decided here.
export pure func redaction_is_reportable(r: Redaction) -> bool {
  match r.impact {
    RedactionChangesTrajectory => true,
    RedactionDemoted => false
  }
}

-- The replacement text. It names the RULE, so a reader of a redacted artifact
-- can tell why the value is gone, and it is deliberately outside every
-- detector's own alphabet — a marker that re-triggered the scan would make
-- redaction non-idempotent and the second pass would report findings that the
-- first pass created.
export pure func redaction_marker(rule: string) -> string {
  "<redacted ${rule}>"
}

pure func redact_if(site: SecretSite, s: string) -> string {
  match scan_text(site, s) {
    [] => s,
    f :: _ => redaction_marker(secret_rule(f))
  }
}

export type RedactedEnvMap = {
  entries: [{ key: string, value: string }],
  redactions: [Redaction]
}

pure func redact_env_entry(e: { key: string, value: string }) -> RedactedEnvMap {
  match scan_env_entry(e) {
    [] => { entries: [e], redactions: [] },
    f :: _ =>
      { entries: [{ key: e.key, value: redaction_marker(secret_rule(f)) }],
        redactions: [{ finding: f, impact: site_impact(f.site) }] }
  }
}

export pure func redact_env_map(es: [{ key: string, value: string }]) -> RedactedEnvMap {
  match es {
    [] => { entries: [], redactions: [] },
    e :: rest => {
      let head = redact_env_entry(e);
      let tail = redact_env_map(rest);
      { entries: head.entries ++ tail.entries,
        redactions: head.redactions ++ tail.redactions }
    }
  }
}

export type RedactedInteractions = {
  interactions: [Interaction],
  redactions: [Redaction]
}

pure func redact_chunks(ord: int, cs: [StreamChunkRecord], n: int) -> [StreamChunkRecord] {
  match cs {
    [] => [],
    c :: rest =>
      [{ offset_ms: c.offset_ms, text: redact_if(ChunkSite(ord, n), c.text) }]
        ++ redact_chunks(ord, rest, n + 1)
  }
}

-- The IDENTITY is deliberately NOT redacted.
--
-- Both readings type-check and the wrong one is silent, so the choice is
-- recorded here. Redacting an identity component replaces the thing replay
-- compares on: `identity_body_eq` then reports every position as a causal
-- mismatch, which strict replay treats as fatal — so a redacted program does
-- not replay at all, and the artifact preserved for a failure no longer
-- reproduces it. Leaving it makes the FINDING survive, and a credential in an
-- identity is a defect in the recorder that must be fixed at the recorder.
-- `scan_interaction` still reports it, so `encode_program` still refuses; what
-- is refused here is the pretence that redaction fixed it.
pure func redact_interaction(i: Interaction) -> RedactedInteractions {
  let o = i.identity.ordinal;
  let rq = redact_if(RequestProjectionSite(o), i.request_projection);
  let pl = redact_if(PayloadSite(o), i.outcome.payload);
  let cs = redact_chunks(o, i.outcome.chunks, 0);
  let found = scan_text(RequestProjectionSite(o), i.request_projection)
                ++ scan_text(PayloadSite(o), i.outcome.payload)
                ++ scan_chunks(o, i.outcome.chunks, 0);
  { interactions: [{ identity: i.identity,
                     request_projection: rq,
                     deadline_ms: i.deadline_ms,
                     outcome: { i.outcome | payload: pl, chunks: cs } }],
    redactions: as_redactions(found) }
}

pure func as_redactions(fs: [SecretFinding]) -> [Redaction] {
  match fs {
    [] => [],
    f :: rest => [{ finding: f, impact: site_impact(f.site) }] ++ as_redactions(rest)
  }
}

export pure func redact_interactions(xs: [Interaction]) -> RedactedInteractions {
  match xs {
    [] => { interactions: [], redactions: [] },
    i :: rest => {
      let head = redact_interaction(i);
      let tail = redact_interactions(rest);
      { interactions: head.interactions ++ tail.interactions,
        redactions: head.redactions ++ tail.redactions }
    }
  }
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Query helpers for the acceptance suite
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Every rule this detector can produce, in a fixed order, DERIVED from the
-- reason constructors rather than written out.
--
-- Stage 2's `all_interaction_kinds()` established the pattern and the reason is
-- the same one: the acceptance suite asserts that its fixture set reaches every
-- rule in this list, so a detector variant added here and reached by no fixture
-- turns that row red instead of shipping as a branch nobody has ever executed.
-- S8's complement in its cheapest form — an unwalked branch is not pinned, it
-- is absent, and absent reads identically to unchanged.
export pure func all_secret_rules() -> [string] {
  [secret_rule({ site: EnvKeySite(""), reason: CredentialBearingName("") }),
   secret_rule({ site: EnvKeySite(""), reason: ProviderTokenLiteral("") }),
   secret_rule({ site: EnvKeySite(""), reason: UrlUserinfo }),
   secret_rule({ site: EnvKeySite(""), reason: PrivateKeyBlock }),
   secret_rule({ site: EnvKeySite(""), reason: JsonWebToken }),
   secret_rule({ site: EnvKeySite(""), reason: OpaqueHighEntropy(0) })]
}

export pure func has_secret_rule(fs: [SecretFinding], rule: string) -> bool {
  match fs {
    [] => false,
    f :: rest => if secret_rule(f) == rule then true else has_secret_rule(rest, rule)
  }
}

export pure func secret_rules(fs: [SecretFinding]) -> [string] {
  match fs {
    [] => [],
    f :: rest => secret_rule(f) :: secret_rules(rest)
  }
}

export pure func reportable_redactions(rs: [Redaction]) -> [Redaction] {
  match rs {
    [] => [],
    r :: rest =>
      if redaction_is_reportable(r) then r :: reportable_redactions(rest)
      else reportable_redactions(rest)
  }
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Inline tests
--
-- The FIXTURES live in scripts/dst/program_persistence_dst.ail, where a whole
-- program can be mutated one field at a time and where the driver's own seven
-- env keys are in scope. What is tested here is the part with no fixture: the
-- detectors themselves, and in particular the ones whose FALSE POSITIVES would
-- reject every honest artifact.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- The negative control, and it is the load-bearing test in this module.
--
-- Every manifest this project persists carries a git revision and this module's
-- own artifacts carry sha256 digests. Both are long unbroken runs in the
-- credential alphabet. If `longest_opaque_run` scored them, `encode_program`
-- would refuse every honest program and the whole persistence path would be
-- unusable — and it would look like the detector working.
pure func test_a_revision_and_a_digest_are_not_high_entropy_secrets() -> bool
  tests [((), true)]
  {
    let sha = "2cf24dba5fb0a30e26e83b2ac5b9e29e1b161e5c1fa7425e73043362938b9824";
    let rev = "3b52a24d24431c372ed5605289ef039592209514";
    longest_opaque_run(sha) == 0
      && longest_opaque_run(rev) == 0
      && List.length(scan_text(InitialWorldSite("manifest"), sha)) == 0
      && List.length(scan_text(InitialWorldSite("manifest"), rev)) == 0
  }

-- ...and the positive half, on the same detector. A test that only proved the
-- absence of false positives is passed perfectly by a detector that never
-- fires.
pure func test_a_mixed_case_opaque_token_is_high_entropy() -> bool
  tests [((), true)]
  {
    let tok = "Zk3Rj9QpLm2XtVb7Nc4Hs8Wd1Fg6Ya0Ue5Ti";
    longest_opaque_run(tok) >= opaque_run_threshold()
      && has_secret_rule(scan_text(EnvKeySite("MOTOKO_SESSION_ID"), tok),
                         "secret-opaque-high-entropy")
  }

-- URL userinfo, both directions. The negative half is the one that matters:
-- `https://api.example.com/v1` is what `OPENAI_BASE_URL` normally holds, and a
-- detector that flags it makes the driver's own configuration unpersistable.
pure func test_url_userinfo_discriminates_a_credential_from_a_plain_url() -> bool
  tests [((), true)]
  {
    has_url_userinfo("https://svc:s3cr3tvalue@api.example.com/v1")
      && not has_url_userinfo("https://api.example.com/v1")
      -- A bare username is not a credential and D8 does not protect it.
      && not has_url_userinfo("https://user@api.example.com/v1")
      -- An "@" in the PATH is not userinfo. Without the authority-section
      -- restriction this is a false positive on an ordinary URL.
      && not has_url_userinfo("https://api.example.com/v1/users/a:b@example.com")
  }

-- The name axis, and the fact that it fires on the NAME and not on the value:
-- a blank-valued key that announces itself is still a finding, because the
-- persisted map records the key.
pure func test_the_name_axis_reads_the_key_and_the_value_axis_reads_the_value() -> bool
  tests [((), true)]
  {
    let named = scan_env_entry({ key: "SERVICE_API_TOKEN", value: "x" });
    let valued = scan_env_entry({ key: "OPENAI_BASE_URL",
                                  value: "https://svc:tok@api.example.com/v1" });
    has_secret_rule(named, "secret-credential-bearing-name")
      && not has_secret_rule(named, "secret-url-userinfo")
      -- The driver's key is name-clean, so ONLY the value axis reaches it.
      && has_secret_rule(valued, "secret-url-userinfo")
      && not has_secret_rule(valued, "secret-credential-bearing-name")
  }

-- Redaction is idempotent, which is what the marker's alphabet buys. A second
-- pass reporting findings the first pass created would make the reportable set
-- depend on how many times the path ran.
pure func test_redaction_is_idempotent() -> bool
  tests [((), true)]
  {
    let once = redact_env_map([{ key: "OPENAI_BASE_URL",
                                 value: "https://svc:tok@api.example.com/v1" }]);
    let twice = redact_env_map(once.entries);
    List.length(once.redactions) == 1
      && List.length(twice.redactions) == 0
      && List.length(scan_env_map(once.entries)) == 0
  }

-- The three detectors no other row in this module walks, both directions each.
--
-- They are here rather than only in the acceptance suite because each carries a
-- false-positive risk that would reject honest artifacts: "sk-" appears inside
-- ordinary words, "-----BEGIN" appears on a PUBLIC key too, and a dotted
-- version string has the shape of a JWT without the header prefix.
pure func test_the_prefix_pem_and_jwt_detectors_discriminate() -> bool
  tests [((), true)]
  {
    let site = PayloadSite(0);
    has_secret_rule(scan_text(site, "sk-live-AAAA1111BBBB2222"), "secret-provider-token-literal")
      && has_secret_rule(scan_text(site, "AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE"), "secret-provider-token-literal")
      && has_secret_rule(scan_text(site, "authorization: Bearer abc.def"), "secret-provider-token-literal")
      && has_secret_rule(
           scan_text(site, "-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\nMIIB\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----"),
           "secret-private-key-block")
      -- A PUBLIC key is not a credential, and a rule keyed on "-----BEGIN"
      -- alone would redact every certificate a program legitimately carries.
      && not has_secret_rule(
           scan_text(site, "-----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----\nMIIB\n-----END PUBLIC KEY-----"),
           "secret-private-key-block")
      && has_secret_rule(scan_text(site, "eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9.eyJzdWIiOiIxMjM0NTY3ODkwIn0.dBjftJeZ4CVPmB92K27uhbUJU1p1r1wa"),
                         "secret-json-web-token")
      -- Three dot-separated segments that are not a JWT: a version string, and
      -- an ordinary sentence. Both would trip a segment-count-only rule.
      && not has_secret_rule(scan_text(site, "ailang 0.26.0 pinned"), "secret-json-web-token")
      && not has_secret_rule(scan_text(site, "one.two.three"), "secret-json-web-token")
      -- ...and the two controls that CARRY the prefix, which the four above do
      -- not. Without these the segment rules are branches this row claims to
      -- cover and never enters: reducing `has_jwt` to `contains(s, "eyJ")`
      -- left every row in this module and every row in the acceptance suite
      -- green, which is S8's complement arriving inside its own test.
      && not has_secret_rule(
           scan_text(site, "the header decodes to eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9 and nothing follows"),
           "secret-json-web-token")
      -- Three segments and the right prefix, but the payload and signature are
      -- far too short to be either. This is what the length clause is for.
      && not has_secret_rule(scan_text(site, "eyJ.a.b"), "secret-json-web-token")
  }

-- The tension carried in the type, asserted rather than described. An
-- environment value is a replay input and a request projection is not, so the
-- two must classify differently — if they ever collapse to one value, the
-- persistence path stops being able to report which redactions cost a
-- trajectory.
pure func test_the_two_replay_impacts_are_distinct_and_correctly_assigned() -> bool
  tests [((), true)]
  {
    replay_impact_id(site_impact(EnvKeySite("k"))) == "changes-trajectory"
      && replay_impact_id(site_impact(PayloadSite(0))) == "changes-trajectory"
      && replay_impact_id(site_impact(IdentityComponentSite(0, "c"))) == "changes-trajectory"
      && replay_impact_id(site_impact(RequestProjectionSite(0))) == "demoted"
      && redaction_is_reportable({ finding: { site: EnvKeySite("k"), reason: UrlUserinfo },
                                   impact: site_impact(EnvKeySite("k")) })
      && not redaction_is_reportable({ finding: { site: RequestProjectionSite(0),
                                                  reason: UrlUserinfo },
                                       impact: site_impact(RequestProjectionSite(0)) })
  }
